%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: e9620f8c34305754d8cc9a7e49e852d9, type: 3}
  m_Name: Chapter 7 text_en
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Localization::UnityEngine.Localization.Tables.StringTable
  m_LocaleId:
    m_Code: en
  m_SharedData: {fileID: 11400000, guid: c6da5e72456f23d428a98d0c5ad5c931, type: 2}
  m_Metadata:
    m_Items: []
  m_TableData:
  - m_Id: 43523002368
    m_Localized: 'The first bit of news: the <color=orange>processor</color> is so
      huge that the <color=yellow>miracle feature</color> with <color=#48DBFB>camera
      control</color> is back, so you can take a detailed look at everything.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1078857584640
    m_Localized: 'The second bit of news: there''s a <color=orange>new panel in the
      UI</color> (click the <color=yellow>arrow on the right</color> to see it).
      It gives you hints about which <color=yellow>phase of instruction processing</color>
      we''re currently in and what''s normally supposed to happen in this <color=yellow>state</color>.
      But hey, of course you can also just ignore the panel.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1090916208640
    m_Localized: 'Otherwise, my tip: first think about which <color=orange>instruction</color>
      you''re currently processing and what it actually does (load something into
      <color=orange>memory</color> or add two numbers?).'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1101422940160
    m_Localized: 'Then think about how you get to the right instruction. And then:
      in how many steps is this instruction processed? Just take a look at the <color=orange>finite
      state machine</color> for that. As many <color=yellow>states</color> as the
      path has, that''s how many <color=#48DBFB>clock cycles</color> you need for
      processing.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1117730394112
    m_Localized: Don't be alarmed if the <color=yellow>state number</color> jumps
      back and forth. That's completely normal. Take a look at the state machine
      from the <color=orange>slides</color>, then you'll see that you're just following
      the chain.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1128593641472
    m_Localized: 'And remember: just making it this far is a <anim:wave><color=yellow>great
      achievement</color></anim>. I''m proud of you!'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1137661726720
    m_Localized: <anim:wave><color=yellow>Good luck!</color></anim>
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1147979714560
    m_Localized: <anim:wave>I'm here to chew bubble gum and tell you about the new
      levels...</anim> <anim:shake><color=red>and I'm all out of gum</color></anim>.
      So I guess we'll go straight to the second one.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1968050032640
    m_Localized: "We've already come across this instruction before, but not in its
      normal form - rather \u201Ein individual pieces\u201C."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1993148747776
    m_Localized: Do you remember how we addressed the <color=orange>RegisterFile</color>?
      We only needed the <color=yellow>register addresses</color> and the address
      of the <color=yellow>target register</color>, right? Well, basically that's
      exactly what we just described for <color=orange>R-Type Instructions</color>
      Instructions. So I think you'll have <anim:wave>absolutely no problems</anim>
      with this level!
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2137999036416
    m_Localized: <anim:wave>This time you'll have to work a bit harder</anim> before
      you start the task.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2175970070528
    m_Localized: Before, the <color=yellow>PC (Program Counter)</color> directly
      matched the instruction address, and there wasn't much to do (just take the
      value from the <color=yellow>PC</color> and transfer it to <color=orange>memory</color>).
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2185419837440
    m_Localized: But if you now look at the <color=orange>side panel</color>, you'll
      see that it's showing error messages because it <color=red>can't interpret</color>
      the current state.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2196564103168
    m_Localized: That's because we have to set the <color=yellow>PC</color> to the
      <color=green>corresponding address</color> before we start processing the instruction!
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2995251859456
    m_Localized: <anim:wave><color=yellow>We don't just want to add numbers, after
      all :D</color></anim>
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3019507519488
    m_Localized: This time our goal is - to store a value in memory!
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3168002658304
    m_Localized: As you may have noticed, there's no classic <color=orange>if statement</color>
      in <color=#48DBFB>Assembly</color>.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3268468822016
    m_Localized: All <color=#48DBFB>branches</color> are implemented by comparing
      <color=yellow>register values</color>.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3290212093952
    m_Localized: A <color=orange>branch instruction</color> makes the processor jump
      to a specific <color=yellow>address</color> (or to a <color=yellow>label</color>
      that represents an offset).
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3304703414272
    m_Localized: So that this jump is <anim:wave><color=yellow>"intelligent"</color></anim>
      and only happens under certain conditions, we use <color=orange>conditional
      jump instructions</color>.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3323774914560
    m_Localized: Do you know how conditional branches work in <color=yellow>RISC-V</color>?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3346516430848
    m_Localized: In <color=yellow>RISC-V</color> the <color=#48DBFB>"if... then..."
      logic</color> is built directly into the instruction.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3359501996032
    m_Localized: 'Here are a few examples:

      - <color=orange>beq</color> (Branch
      if Equal): Jump if both numbers are <anim:shake><color=green>equal</color></anim>.

      -
      <color=orange>blt</color> (Branch if Less Than): Jump if the first number is
      <anim:shake><color=green>smaller</color></anim>.

      - <color=orange>bne</color>
      (Branch if Not Equal): Jump if the numbers are <anim:shake><color=red>not equal</color></anim>.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3408650850304
    m_Localized: <anim:wave><color=yellow>Good luck!</color></anim>
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 4528467103744
    m_Localized: Oh, this operation is <anim:wave><color=yellow>absolutely essential</color></anim>
      if you want to write <color=#48DBFB>subroutines (methods)</color> in your assembly
      code!
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 4562109616128
    m_Localized: It performs a <color=orange>jump</color> to an address (similar
      to a branch), but at the same time stores the <color=yellow>address of the
      next line</color>, so you can <color=green>return</color> and continue after
      the method has been executed.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 4571332890624
    m_Localized: More details about this were probably covered in the lecture, but
      keep this concept in mind when you're controlling the <color=orange>control
      bits</color>.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 4580174483456
    m_Localized: <anim:wave><color=yellow>Good luck!</color></anim>
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3383929622528
    m_Localized: 'This can be interpreted as follows:

      - <color=orange>beq</color>:
      If <color=#48DBFB>x1 - x3 == 0</color>, the jump happens.

      - <color=orange>blt</color>:
      If <color=#48DBFB>x1 - x3 < 0</color>, the jump happens.

      - <color=orange>bne</color>:
      If the result is <color=red>not zero</color>, the jump happens.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3396407676928
    m_Localized: 'And here''s how it''s written:

      <color=orange>beq</color>
      <color=yellow>x1, x3, target</color>  <color=green># If x1 == x3, jump to "target"</color>

      <color=orange>addi</color>
      <color=yellow>x1, x1, 1</color>      <color=green># Executed if x1 != x3</color>

      <color=yellow>target:</color>'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 11314720952320
    m_Localized: Stuck? Yeah, I understand - this <color=yellow>circuit</color> really
      is <anim:shake><color=red>huge</color></anim>!
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 11431301632000
    m_Localized: On the right side there's an <color=yellow>arrow</color>. If you
      click on it, a <color=#48DBFB>hint</color> appears!
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 11444664684544
    m_Localized: 'There the current <color=yellow>state of the processor</color>
      is described: what this state''s job is and which <color=#48DBFB>signals</color>
      need to be set!'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 11454047342592
    m_Localized: 'Just one thing: the <color=#48DBFB>signals</color> don''t always
      mean exactly what it says in the hints (just like in the slides or the exam''s
      reference sheet).

      For example, <color=#48DBFB>AluOp = 00</color> isn''t
      addition, but an <color=yellow>arithmetic operation (Type I)</color> according
      to the <color=yellow>RISC-V reference</color>.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 11465208385536
    m_Localized: But if you keep all that in mind, I'm sure you'll <anim:wave><color=yellow>manage
      it :D</color></anim>!
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2112043287859200
    m_Localized: Process the value from Data/Instruction Memory at address 4 correctly.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2112083737726976
    m_Localized: Process the value from Data/Instruction Memory at address 0 correctly.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2112092491239424
    m_Localized: Process the value from Data/Instruction Memory at address 8 correctly.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2112102146527232
    m_Localized: Process the value from Data/Instruction Memory at address 0 correctly.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2112110765821952
    m_Localized: Process the value from Data/Instruction Memory at address 12 correctly.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2112124065959936
    m_Localized: Process the value from Data/Instruction Memory at address 12 correctly.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  references:
    version: 2
    RefIds: []
